{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a paper submitted to AAAI. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate experimental design, statistical methodology, results validity, and limitations.\n\n## Scoring Scale (AAAI 1-10)\n1=trivial/wrong, 2=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 4=reject, 5=marginally below threshold, 6=marginally above threshold, 7=good paper accept, 8=strong accept, 9=top 15%, 10=top 5% seminal\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\n**Core contribution**: First multi-benchmark Generalizability Theory (G-theory) study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision, GPU hardware) with methodology (prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, option ordering) and measurement (test items) factors in LLM evaluation, treating model as a crossed random facet. ~4.3M item-level records, 4 models (7-9B), 5 benchmarks, 2 GPU types.\n\n**9 Experiments**:\n\n**Exp 1: Full-Scale 4-Model MMLU G-Study**\n- Design: 4 models × 3 precisions (Gemma: 2) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items = 891,200 records\n- Uses vLLM 0.9.2 with enforce_eager=True\n- Single-model: item_id dominates (mean 60.4%, range 58.7-62.3%)\n- Cross-model: model×item = 24.1% [95% CI: 21.3, 27.0], item_id = 36.8% [33.5, 40.2]\n- G collapse: single-model mean G=0.618 → 4-model cross G=0.065; D-study 10 models G=0.179\n\n**Exp 2: Item Resampling Stability**\n- 3 independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples (seeds 0, 7, 99) × Llama × 3 prec × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 10,800 records/sample\n- item_id CV = 0.039 (well below 15% threshold); G CV = 0.030\n- Rank order preserved across all 3 samples (Friedman test p > 0.10)\n\n**Exp 3: Cross-Benchmark Variance Gradient**\n- 4 models × 2 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings × 200 items on HellaSwag, ARC-C, GSM8K (1,382,400 new records)\n- Monotonically decreasing item_id: MMLU(60.4%) → HellaSwag(50.4%) → ARC-C(42.1%) → GSM8K(28.3%); Spearman ρ=1.0\n- Monotonically increasing prompt×item: 3.2% → 5.4% → 7.3% → 11.8%\n- model×item persists 21.3-24.1% across all benchmarks\n- D-study budgets: MMLU 480 items × 3 prompts × 4 seeds; GSM8K 900 × 4 × 6\n\n**Exp 4: Multi-Hardware Separability**\n- 4 models × 2 GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell + second) × matched design; 1,382,400 total records\n- hardware×methodology (aggregate) = 0.18% [CI: 0.03, 0.42], well below 0.5% threshold\n- All individual hw×facet interactions < 0.1% except hw×item = 1.2% [0.7, 1.8]\n- Metric-conditional: hw×temp binary=0.04% vs text-exact-match=0.83%\n\n**Exp 5: Comprehensive Combined Analysis** (purely analytical, 0 GPU hours)\n- 17 systematic analyses on all 4.29M records\n- IRT vs G-theory: G-theory requires 4.4× more items (MMLU: 510 vs 116); G-theory G at IRT recommendation = 0.529\n- Bootstrap CIs: model×item [21.3, 27.0] excludes zero; infra×method [0.0, 0.42] includes zero\n- 9 methodological defenses (MD1-MD9) all pass\n- REML vs Henderson III: max divergence 1.4%, rank order preserved\n- Logprob scoring: item_id = 53.2% (vs 60.4% binary), cross-metric rank correlation 0.94\n- Higher-order interactions: 3.2% total; largest = prec×temp×item = 1.1%\n- Power analysis: >0.998 for ≥5% interaction effects; MDE at 80% power = 1.2%\n- G at full 14K MMLU = 0.943\n\n**Exp 6: 14B Model Scale Extension**\n- Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU, 345,600 records\n- item_id: 62.5% (14B) vs 60.4% (8B), +2.1pp non-significant\n- Variance hierarchy fully preserved; model×item between 14B models = 22.4%\n\n**Exp 7: Fine-Tuning G-Study**\n- RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA: 6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 = 1,728 runs\n- Regime change: seed×lr = 28.4% (#1) vs inference item_id = 60.4% (#1)\n- Fine-tuning item_id = 31.7% (#2); gpu×precision = 0.4%\n- Task-specific: SST-2 item_id lowest (24.8%, easiest) vs RTE highest (38.2%, hardest)\n\n**Exp 8: LayerCast Precision Mitigation**\n- LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as 4th precision level on MMLU, Llama, 86,400 records\n- Precision main: 1.4% → 0.5% (−64%); precision×item: 2.8% → 1.0% (−64%)\n- G: 0.618 → 0.641 (+0.023); modest because precision terms are only ~4% of total\n\n**Exp 9: MATH Benchmark Extension**\n- 4 models × 2 prec × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings × 200 MATH items = 460,800 records\n- item_id = 22.1% (lowest), temp×item = 14.2%, prompt×item = 13.5%, residual = 24.6%\n- Extended gradient: perfect monotonicity ρ=1.0, spread 38.3pp\n- D-study: 1,150 items for G≥0.80 (2.4× MMLU)\n- model×item = 24.8% (highest across all benchmarks)\n\n**Analysis Pipeline**: Henderson's Method III for balanced designs, REML for unbalanced. Bootstrap CIs with 1,000 resamples. D-study grid search targeting G≥0.80.\n\n**Tables**: 18 tables total (Tables 1-18), covering variance decomposition, G-coefficients, bootstrap CIs, IRT comparison, D-study budgets, GPU cost estimates, methodological defenses, etc.\n\n**Stated Limitations**: (1) Binary G-theory assumes continuous responses; mitigated by null simulations (671×) and logprob analyses. (2) Temperature and ordering have limited design points (3 and 4); fixed-vs-random sensitivity <3%. (3) Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa; decoder fine-tuning deferred. (4) Only open-weight models. (5) 14B models require tensor parallelism introducing additional nondeterminism.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a thorough R1 review covering:\n\n1. **Experimental Design Rigor**: Are designs appropriate? Is the factorial structure well-motivated? Are sample sizes adequate?\n2. **Statistical Methodology**: Is G-theory correctly applied? Are Henderson III/REML appropriate? Are bootstrap CIs valid? Is the power analysis convincing?\n3. **Results Validity**: Do the results support the claims? Are confidence intervals appropriately interpreted? Are effect sizes meaningful?\n4. **Methodological Defenses**: Are the 9 defenses (MD1-MD9) sufficient to address potential criticisms?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is the experimental setup described well enough to reproduce?\n6. **Limitations**: Are limitations honestly stated? Are there unstated limitations?\n7. **Specific Concerns**: Any methodological red flags?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with brief elaboration]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with brief elaboration]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/10\n- Experimental Design: X/10\n- Reproducibility: X/10\n- Overall (R1): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GAXTVtMNSxK9HadZ66bcKm","session_id":"7653c71b-c80c-411e-b242-de88d6b84f6d","uuid":"80db54d4-c6c1-4395-9f64-e6ecb48c32f9","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:46:48.983Z"}
