{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a63668dee0181e2d4","tool_use_id":"toolu_011a786N7e3BAGE6Aq8qDMf6","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a peer review of a paper submitted to AAAI. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate the originality of contributions, positioning against prior work, significance, and writing quality.\n\n## Scoring Scale (AAAI 1-10)\n1=trivial/wrong, 2=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 4=reject, 5=marginally below threshold, 6=marginally above threshold, 7=good paper accept, 8=strong accept, 9=top 15%, 10=top 5% seminal\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\n**Core**: First multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure × methodology × measurement in LLM evaluation. ~4.3M item-level records, 4 models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B) + 2 14B models + RoBERTa-large, 5 benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag, ARC-C, GSM8K, MATH), 2 GPU architectures, 9 experiments.\n\n**Four Claimed Contributions**:\n1. **Model×item interaction as hidden dominant source**: When model is a crossed facet, model×item=24.1% on MMLU, collapsing cross-model G from 0.618 to 0.065. First G-theory quantification of cross-model item-level disagreement.\n2. **Confirmatory separability with metric qualification**: Infrastructure×methodology <0.5% under binary scoring; but metric-conditional—text-exact-match hw×temp reaches 0.83%.\n3. **IRT vs G-theory cost**: G-theory requires 4.4× more items than IRT because it accounts for prompt, seed, ordering dimensions that IRT ignores.\n4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory pipeline with benchmark-specific D-study budget tables, temperature-stratified reliability guides, model-as-facet analysis templates.\n\n**Key Findings**:\n- Item dominance follows monotonically decreasing gradient: MMLU(60.4%) → HellaSwag(50.4%) → ARC-C(42.1%) → GSM8K(28.3%) → MATH(22.1%), Spearman ρ=1.0 against cognitive complexity\n- model×item persists at 21-25% across ALL benchmarks—irreducible barrier to cross-model generalizability\n- D-study projections: MMLU needs 480 items, GSM8K needs 900 items for G≥0.80\n- Fine-tuning reveals qualitative regime change: seed×lr (28.4%) supplants item_id (31.7%) as dominant source\n\n**Related Work Section** covers three strands:\n1. **Variance in ML evaluation**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) ANOVA for ML benchmarks; Henderson et al. (2018) on RL; Dodge et al. (2020) and Sellam et al. (2022) on fine-tuning instability; Dehghani et al. (2021) on benchmark lottery\n2. **LLM evaluation variance**: Yuan et al. (2025) on numerical precision (9%); ThinkingMachines (2025) on batch-invariance; Messina & Scotta (2026) on background randomness; Brittlebench (2026) on prompt sensitivity (50%); Hua et al. (2025) on evaluation artifacts; Lu et al. (2022) and Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) on option ordering\n3. **Psychometric approaches**: Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to LLM eval with 5 facets on single benchmark; IRT work by Lalor et al. (2019), Rodriguez et al. (2021), Vania et al. (2021), Polo et al. (2024); Perlitz et al. (2023) on efficient benchmarking\n\nPaper claims to differ by: (a) crossing infrastructure and methodology, (b) comparing variance structure across 5 benchmarks, (c) providing metric-conditional, benchmark-specific reproducibility budgets.\n\n**References**: ~20 citations total (Bouthillier 2021, Brennan 2001, Brittlebench 2026, Cronbach 1972, Dehghani 2021, Dodge 2020, Henderson 2018, Hua 2025, Kunievsky 2025, Lalor 2019, Lu 2022, Mesting 2026, Messina & Scotta 2026, Perlitz 2023, Pezeshkpour & Hruschka 2024, Polo 2024, Rodriguez 2021, Sellam 2022, Shavelson & Webb 1991, ThinkingMachines 2025, Vania 2021, Yuan 2025, Biderman 2024)\n\n**Paper Structure**: 13 pages. Abstract, Intro (2 pages), Related Work (1 page), Background: G-theory (0.5 page), Experimental Design (1.5 pages), Results spanning 9 experiments (5 pages), Discussion (1.5 pages), Conclusion (0.5 page), References (1.5 pages). 18 tables, figures described but not shown in text.\n\n**Limitations stated**: (1) Binary G-theory assumes continuous; (2) Limited temperature/ordering design points; (3) Encoder-only fine-tuning only; (4) Only open-weight models; (5) 14B tensor parallelism nondeterminism.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a thorough R2 review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty**: How novel are the four contributions? Is this genuinely new or incremental over Mesting (2026)?\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Significance and Impact**: Will this change how the community evaluates LLMs? Are the practical recommendations actionable?\n4. **Differentiation from Prior Work**: Is the claimed advance over Mesting (2026), Yuan et al. (2025), Brittlebench (2026) etc. convincing?\n5. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Is the narrative clear? Is the abstract accurate?\n6. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**: Do the claims match what the experiments actually show?\n7. **Scope and Ambition**: Is the scope appropriate for a top venue? Is it too narrow or too broad?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with brief elaboration]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with brief elaboration]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Writing Quality: X/10\n- Overall (R2): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n","uuid":"e5f68809-1b5c-44a3-bbb3-895e8bbf5b50","session_id":"7653c71b-c80c-411e-b242-de88d6b84f6d"}
