{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GAXTVtMNSxK9HadZ66bcKm","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed to write a thorough R1 review focused on Methods and Experiments. Let me compose it.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to decompose variance in LLM evaluation across infrastructure (numerical precision, GPU hardware), methodology (prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, option ordering), and measurement (test items) facets, with model treated as a crossed random facet. Nine experiments totaling ~4.3M item-level records across 4 models (7-9B), 2 models (14B), 5 benchmarks, and 2 GPU types yield three claims: (1) item selection dominates single-model variance but model-by-item interaction emerges as the second-largest source in cross-model analysis, with a monotonic cross-benchmark gradient; (2) infrastructure-methodology separability holds under binary scoring on knowledge benchmarks; (3) reproducibility budgets are benchmark-specific and metric-conditional, with G-theory requiring 4.4x more items than IRT. The experimental scope is ambitious and the methodological defense package is unusually thorough for this literature.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated factorial design.** The fully crossed design on MMLU (4 models x 3 precisions x 6 prompts x 6 seeds x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings x 200 items) is the most comprehensive variance decomposition in the LLM evaluation literature. The decision to cross infrastructure with methodology factors -- rather than studying them in isolation as all prior work has done -- directly tests an assumption the field has been making implicitly. This is sound experimental logic.\n\n2. **Appropriate statistical framework with correct implementation.** Henderson's Method III for balanced designs and REML for unbalanced designs are the right choices. The code (`variance_decomposition.py`) correctly implements the EMS equations: 2-way interactions are estimated first (MS_ij - MS_res divided by the product of levels of all other facets), then main effects are estimated accounting for interaction contributions. The negative-variance truncation to zero is standard practice. The D-study partition (fixed facets to universe score, random facets to error) is correctly implemented in `d_study.py`.\n\n3. **Comprehensive sensitivity and robustness analyses (MD1-MD9).** The 9 methodological defenses address the most predictable criticisms with quantitative evidence rather than hand-waving. Particularly strong: (a) the null Bernoulli simulation showing item_id = 0.08% vs. observed 61.1% (671x ratio), which rules out the mechanical variance explanation; (b) the REML vs. Henderson III comparison (max divergence 1.4%, rank order preserved); (c) the bootstrap vs. jackknife CI comparison (width ratio 0.96-1.04); (d) the fixed vs. random sensitivity analysis (G ranges 0.618-0.664). This is an exemplary robustness package.\n\n4. **Item resampling stability (Exp 2) directly addresses a critical threat.** Three independent stratified 200-item draws with item_id CV = 0.039 (well below the 15% threshold) and preserved rank order (Friedman p > 0.10) is strong evidence that the headline findings are not artifacts of the specific item sample. This experiment alone addresses one of the most common criticisms of subsampled evaluation studies.\n\n5. **Cross-benchmark gradient is convincing and explanatory.** The monotonic decrease of item_id from MMLU (60.4%) to MATH (22.1%) with Spearman rho = 1.0 across 5 benchmarks, accompanied by the inverse monotonic increase of prompt-by-item interaction (3.2% to 13.5%), provides an elegant reconciliation of seemingly contradictory findings in the literature. The 38.3pp spread is substantial and the pattern is internally consistent.\n\n6. **Power analysis preempts the \"underpowered\" criticism.** Power > 0.998 for effects >= 5% and MDE = 1.2% at 80% power, given the 891,200-record MMLU design, demonstrates that the near-zero infrastructure-methodology interactions are genuine nulls rather than failures to detect.\n\n7. **Model-as-facet analysis (Exp 1 cross-model) is a genuine methodological contribution.** The demonstration that item_id drops from 60.4% (single-model mean) to 36.8% (cross-model) while model-by-item absorbs 24.1% -- and that this collapses G from 0.618 to 0.065 -- is a clean and important finding. The D-study showing even 10 models yield G = 0.179 quantifies the irreducibility of this interaction.\n\n8. **The IRT vs. G-theory comparison (Exp 5) is a standalone contribution.** Showing that G-theory requires 4.4x more items (510 vs. 116) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores, and that G-theory G at IRT's recommended items is only 0.529, directly informs practitioners currently using tinyBenchmarks-style approaches.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Four models is marginal for random-effects estimation of the model facet (Major).** The model-as-facet analysis -- a core contribution -- estimates model-by-item variance from only 4 models (Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B). In G-theory practice, variance components from facets with fewer than 5-6 levels are known to be unstable and positively biased (Brennan, 2001, Ch. 4). The 95% CI on model-by-item [21.3, 27.0] is reasonably tight, but this tightness reflects bootstrap resampling over items, not over models -- the 4 models are fixed across all bootstrap samples. The actual uncertainty in the model-by-item estimate due to model sampling is not quantified. A leave-one-model-out analysis would partially address this: if removing any single model substantially changes the item_id / model-by-item ratio, the df=3 concern is not merely theoretical. The 14B extension (Exp 6) adds 2 models but in a separate analysis, not in the main crossed design, so it cannot strengthen the main estimate.\n\n2. **The \"precision\" facet conflates genuine numerical effects with vLLM engine artifacts (Major).** The paper uses enforce_eager=True for all precisions, which is good for controlling the engine confound discovered in the MVP. However, the memory file reveals that FP32 shows a consistent ~8-10pp accuracy degradation across all models. The paper attributes this to \"weights loaded from BF16 to FP32\" behavior, but this is a vLLM-specific code path issue, not a fundamental property of numerical precision. The precision facet thus has 3 levels where 2 (BF16, FP16) are nearly identical (<0.2pp difference) and 1 (FP32) is dramatically different due to an engine artifact. This means precision-by-item interaction (reported at ~7% on MMLU) is dominated by FP32's anomalous behavior. The LayerCast experiment (Exp 8) partially addresses this by showing precision drops from 1.4% to 0.5% with LayerCast, but the main analyses still use the confounded 3-level precision facet. The paper should present the bf16-vs-fp16-only decomposition as a sensitivity analysis to show how much the precision facet's contribution changes when FP32 is excluded.\n\n3. **Hardware crossing is severely underpowered (Moderate-Major).** Two GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell + one other) provide df=1 for the hardware facet. The reported hardware-by-methodology interaction of 0.18% [0.03, 0.42] is below the 0.5% threshold, but with 2 hardware levels, this estimate has almost no statistical power to detect anything. The key concern is that hardware-by-item = 1.2% [0.7, 1.8], which is non-negligible and exceeds hardware-by-methodology by 6.7x. With only 2 GPU types -- both NVIDIA data-center class -- the separability claim for hardware is much weaker than the paper implies. Cross-architecture effects (NVIDIA vs. AMD, consumer vs. data-center, different microarchitectures) could plausibly be larger. The paper should either (a) present the hardware separability finding as preliminary/suggestive rather than confirmatory, or (b) explicitly scope the claim to \"numerical precision separability\" and defer hardware to future work.\n\n4. **Option ordering facet is undefined for open-ended benchmarks (Moderate).** GSM8K and MATH are open-ended generation tasks without multiple-choice options. The paper reports 4 ordering levels for all benchmarks. What does \"option ordering\" mean for GSM8K and MATH? If it is not applicable and was held constant, then these benchmarks have a 5-facet rather than 6-facet design, which changes the residual interpretation and affects comparability across benchmarks. If some proxy was used (e.g., few-shot example ordering), this must be explicitly stated as it measures a different construct than MC option ordering. This is a non-trivial gap in the experimental description.\n\n5. **Temperature and ordering have too few design points for credible random-facet treatment (Moderate).** Temperature has 3 levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) and ordering has 4 levels (systematic rotations). G-theory requires that facet levels be a random sample from a well-defined universe of admissible conditions. Temperature values are not random draws from a continuous space -- they are selected boundary/midpoint values. The 4 orderings are all possible ABCD rotations, which exhausts the rotation universe but excludes permutations (24 total for 4 options). The sensitivity analysis (fixed vs. random: <3% change) partially mitigates this, but it does not resolve the interpretive problem: what \"universe\" are 3 temperatures generalizing to? The paper should discuss this more carefully in the Methods section and present the fixed-facet analysis as the primary result for these two facets.\n\n6. **D-study grid search procedure is underdocumented (Moderate).** The D-study projections (benchmark-specific budgets for G >= 0.80) are among the paper's most actionable outputs, yet the optimization procedure is not described. What is the objective function? Is it min total items subject to G >= 0.80, or min total conditions (items x prompts x seeds)? What are the search bounds and step sizes? Are costs considered? The `d_study.py` code reveals a simple univariate sweep (vary one facet 1-20, hold others at baseline) with a linear search for minimum replications -- this is not a joint optimization. If the reported budgets come from independent per-facet sweeps rather than joint optimization, the paper should state this explicitly, as jointly optimized allocations could be substantially different.\n\n7. **Cross-benchmark design asymmetry complicates comparison (Minor-Moderate).** MMLU uses 3 precisions x 3 temperatures while other benchmarks use 2 x 2. The paper reports a sensitivity analysis showing <5% change when MMLU is re-analyzed with matched 2x2, but the precision-related components on non-MMLU benchmarks are mechanically smaller because BF16 and FP16 produce nearly identical results (no FP32 in those designs). This means the \"cross-benchmark gradient\" for precision-related components is partly driven by design asymmetry rather than genuine benchmark differences. The item_id gradient is less affected because items vary independently of the precision design.\n\n8. **Gemma2 FP16 exclusion creates an unbalanced design (Minor).** Gemma2 cannot run FP16, so the cross-model analysis uses only BF16+FP32. This means the cross-model design is not fully balanced: Llama/Mistral/Qwen have 3 precision levels while Gemma has 2. Henderson's Method III assumes balanced designs. The paper states REML is used for unbalanced cases, which is correct, but the main MMLU analysis code (`variance_decomposition.py`) implements Henderson Method I (Type-I SS-based), not REML. The paper should clarify which estimator was actually used for the cross-model analysis and verify that the Gemma imbalance does not bias the results.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. What fraction of bootstrap resamples show model-by-item > item_id in the cross-model analysis? The CI on model-by-item [21.3, 27.0] and item_id [33.5, 40.2] do not overlap, which is reassuring, but the relative magnitude matters for the \"second-largest source\" claim.\n\n2. What happens to the variance decomposition if FP32 is excluded from the MMLU analysis, leaving only BF16 vs. FP16? This would isolate \"genuine\" numerical precision effects from the vLLM engine artifact. If precision-by-item drops from ~7% to near-zero, this substantially changes the story.\n\n3. How was option ordering operationalized for GSM8K and MATH? If these benchmarks lack MC options, the ordering facet either measures something different or was not varied, and this must be stated.\n\n4. Leave-one-model-out sensitivity: does removing any single model from the 4-model MMLU analysis change the model-by-item estimate by more than the bootstrap CI width?\n\n5. The D-study budgets assume practitioners will use multiple prompts and seeds. What is the D-study projection for the common practice of 1 prompt, 1 seed, varying only items? This would make the practical gap more concrete.\n\n6. For the fine-tuning experiment (Exp 7): seed-by-LR interaction dominates at 28.4%. Is this driven by specific seed-LR combinations that diverge (e.g., training instability), or is it a smooth interaction? A seed-LR interaction plot would help distinguish these scenarios.\n\n7. The Bernoulli null simulation (MD1) compares null item_id = 0.08% to observed 61.1%. But the relevant comparison for the model-by-item claim is whether a Bernoulli model with model-specific item difficulties (but no systematic model-by-item interaction beyond what Bernoulli mechanics produce) could generate the observed 24.1% model-by-item. Has this been checked?\n\n8. How sensitive is the cross-benchmark gradient to the specific 200-item samples? The item resampling stability test (Exp 2) was done only on MMLU. Given that GSM8K has much lower item_id (28.3%) and higher residual, sampling variability could be larger there.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The variance decomposition code (`variance_decomposition.py`) absorbs all 3-way and higher interactions into the residual. Exp 5 reports that higher-order interactions total 3.2%, with the largest being precision-by-temperature-by-item at 1.1%. It is unclear how this was computed -- the code only fits main effects and 2-way interactions. Was a separate analysis run for higher-order terms? If so, the code does not reflect this.\n\n2. The bootstrap resamples items with replacement but does not resample models. This means bootstrap CIs on model-by-item reflect item sampling uncertainty only, not model sampling uncertainty. This should be stated explicitly.\n\n3. The paper claims \"first multi-benchmark G-theory study\" in LLM evaluation. Verify this against Mesting (2026), who applied G-theory to MMLU and AILuminate (which could be considered multi-benchmark).\n\n4. The `text_exact_match` metric definition uses the most frequent generated text across all conditions as the \"canonical\" answer. This is a somewhat arbitrary reference point that depends on the design itself (adding more conditions could change the canonical text). An alternative would be the T=0, seed=42 output, which is design-independent.\n\n5. Logprob scoring (Exp 5): item_id = 53.2% vs. 60.4% for binary, with cross-metric rank correlation 0.94. This is reported as supporting metric robustness, but the 7.2pp difference in item_id share is non-trivial. The paper should discuss whether this difference is meaningful or within expected variation.\n\n6. The total record count of 4.29M should be broken down by experiment in a summary table for reproducibility. The current presentation makes it difficult to verify the total.\n\n7. Several numbers in the paper summary differ slightly from the project files (e.g., item_id = 60.4% in the summary vs. 61.1% in earlier project files). This likely reflects the transition from 2-model to 4-model single-model averages. Ensure internal consistency.\n\n8. The G coefficient formula should explicitly state whether relative G (Eρ^2) or absolute G (Φ) is used. The D-study code computes relative G (sigma_tau / (sigma_tau + sigma_delta)), which is confirmed by the partition rule, but this should be stated in the Methods.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 7/10\n- Experimental Design: 8/10\n- Reproducibility: 7/10\n- Overall (R1): 7/10\n- Confidence: 4/5 (familiar with G-theory, ANOVA-based variance decomposition, and LLM evaluation methodology)\n\n**Rationale:** The experimental design is the paper's greatest strength -- the factorial crossing of infrastructure with methodology across multiple benchmarks is novel, the scale is impressive, and the sensitivity analyses are exemplary. The statistical methodology is largely correct, with Henderson III and REML properly applied and bootstrap CIs computed. The main concerns are: (a) the model facet has only 4 levels, which is marginal for random-effects estimation of the headline model-by-item finding; (b) the FP32 engine artifact conflates precision effects with software behavior; (c) the hardware crossing with only 2 GPU types is too thin to support separability claims; and (d) the option ordering facet is undefined for open-ended benchmarks. None of these are fatal -- they are addressable through additional analysis (leave-one-model-out, FP32-excluded sensitivity, explicit scoping of hardware claims, and clarification of ordering for open-ended tasks). The cross-benchmark variance gradient, the IRT vs. G-theory comparison, and the model-as-facet demonstration are all well-supported and constitute genuine contributions. The paper sits at the \"good paper, accept\" threshold with required revisions."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a593143090e14f71f (use SendMessage with to: 'a593143090e14f71f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 86120\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 182624</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7653c71b-c80c-411e-b242-de88d6b84f6d","uuid":"7c3f54fd-e42b-43d0-ba8c-7fde97fdc24b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:49:51.608Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a paper submitted to AAAI. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate experimental design, statistical methodology, results validity, and limitations.\n\n## Scoring Scale (AAAI 1-10)\n1=trivial/wrong, 2=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 4=reject, 5=marginally below threshold, 6=marginally above threshold, 7=good paper accept, 8=strong accept, 9=top 15%, 10=top 5% seminal\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\n**Core contribution**: First multi-benchmark Generalizability Theory (G-theory) study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision, GPU hardware) with methodology (prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, option ordering) and measurement (test items) factors in LLM evaluation, treating model as a crossed random facet. ~4.3M item-level records, 4 models (7-9B), 5 benchmarks, 2 GPU types.\n\n**9 Experiments**:\n\n**Exp 1: Full-Scale 4-Model MMLU G-Study**\n- Design: 4 models × 3 precisions (Gemma: 2) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items = 891,200 records\n- Uses vLLM 0.9.2 with enforce_eager=True\n- Single-model: item_id dominates (mean 60.4%, range 58.7-62.3%)\n- Cross-model: model×item = 24.1% [95% CI: 21.3, 27.0], item_id = 36.8% [33.5, 40.2]\n- G collapse: single-model mean G=0.618 → 4-model cross G=0.065; D-study 10 models G=0.179\n\n**Exp 2: Item Resampling Stability**\n- 3 independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples (seeds 0, 7, 99) × Llama × 3 prec × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 10,800 records/sample\n- item_id CV = 0.039 (well below 15% threshold); G CV = 0.030\n- Rank order preserved across all 3 samples (Friedman test p > 0.10)\n\n**Exp 3: Cross-Benchmark Variance Gradient**\n- 4 models × 2 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings × 200 items on HellaSwag, ARC-C, GSM8K (1,382,400 new records)\n- Monotonically decreasing item_id: MMLU(60.4%) → HellaSwag(50.4%) → ARC-C(42.1%) → GSM8K(28.3%); Spearman ρ=1.0\n- Monotonically increasing prompt×item: 3.2% → 5.4% → 7.3% → 11.8%\n- model×item persists 21.3-24.1% across all benchmarks\n- D-study budgets: MMLU 480 items × 3 prompts × 4 seeds; GSM8K 900 × 4 × 6\n\n**Exp 4: Multi-Hardware Separability**\n- 4 models × 2 GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell + second) × matched design; 1,382,400 total records\n- hardware×methodology (aggregate) = 0.18% [CI: 0.03, 0.42], well below 0.5% threshold\n- All individual hw×facet interactions < 0.1% except hw×item = 1.2% [0.7, 1.8]\n- Metric-conditional: hw×temp binary=0.04% vs text-exact-match=0.83%\n\n**Exp 5: Comprehensive Combined Analysis** (purely analytical, 0 GPU hours)\n- 17 systematic analyses on all 4.29M records\n- IRT vs G-theory: G-theory requires 4.4× more items (MMLU: 510 vs 116); G-theory G at IRT recommendation = 0.529\n- Bootstrap CIs: model×item [21.3, 27.0] excludes zero; infra×method [0.0, 0.42] includes zero\n- 9 methodological defenses (MD1-MD9) all pass\n- REML vs Henderson III: max divergence 1.4%, rank order preserved\n- Logprob scoring: item_id = 53.2% (vs 60.4% binary), cross-metric rank correlation 0.94\n- Higher-order interactions: 3.2% total; largest = prec×temp×item = 1.1%\n- Power analysis: >0.998 for ≥5% interaction effects; MDE at 80% power = 1.2%\n- G at full 14K MMLU = 0.943\n\n**Exp 6: 14B Model Scale Extension**\n- Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU, 345,600 records\n- item_id: 62.5% (14B) vs 60.4% (8B), +2.1pp non-significant\n- Variance hierarchy fully preserved; model×item between 14B models = 22.4%\n\n**Exp 7: Fine-Tuning G-Study**\n- RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA: 6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 = 1,728 runs\n- Regime change: seed×lr = 28.4% (#1) vs inference item_id = 60.4% (#1)\n- Fine-tuning item_id = 31.7% (#2); gpu×precision = 0.4%\n- Task-specific: SST-2 item_id lowest (24.8%, easiest) vs RTE highest (38.2%, hardest)\n\n**Exp 8: LayerCast Precision Mitigation**\n- LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as 4th precision level on MMLU, Llama, 86,400 records\n- Precision main: 1.4% → 0.5% (−64%); precision×item: 2.8% → 1.0% (−64%)\n- G: 0.618 → 0.641 (+0.023); modest because precision terms are only ~4% of total\n\n**Exp 9: MATH Benchmark Extension**\n- 4 models × 2 prec × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings × 200 MATH items = 460,800 records\n- item_id = 22.1% (lowest), temp×item = 14.2%, prompt×item = 13.5%, residual = 24.6%\n- Extended gradient: perfect monotonicity ρ=1.0, spread 38.3pp\n- D-study: 1,150 items for G≥0.80 (2.4× MMLU)\n- model×item = 24.8% (highest across all benchmarks)\n\n**Analysis Pipeline**: Henderson's Method III for balanced designs, REML for unbalanced. Bootstrap CIs with 1,000 resamples. D-study grid search targeting G≥0.80.\n\n**Tables**: 18 tables total (Tables 1-18), covering variance decomposition, G-coefficients, bootstrap CIs, IRT comparison, D-study budgets, GPU cost estimates, methodological defenses, etc.\n\n**Stated Limitations**: (1) Binary G-theory assumes continuous responses; mitigated by null simulations (671×) and logprob analyses. (2) Temperature and ordering have limited design points (3 and 4); fixed-vs-random sensitivity <3%. (3) Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa; decoder fine-tuning deferred. (4) Only open-weight models. (5) 14B models require tensor parallelism introducing additional nondeterminism.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a thorough R1 review covering:\n\n1. **Experimental Design Rigor**: Are designs appropriate? Is the factorial structure well-motivated? Are sample sizes adequate?\n2. **Statistical Methodology**: Is G-theory correctly applied? Are Henderson III/REML appropriate? Are bootstrap CIs valid? Is the power analysis convincing?\n3. **Results Validity**: Do the results support the claims? Are confidence intervals appropriately interpreted? Are effect sizes meaningful?\n4. **Methodological Defenses**: Are the 9 defenses (MD1-MD9) sufficient to address potential criticisms?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is the experimental setup described well enough to reproduce?\n6. **Limitations**: Are limitations honestly stated? Are there unstated limitations?\n7. **Specific Concerns**: Any methodological red flags?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with brief elaboration]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with brief elaboration]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/10\n- Experimental Design: X/10\n- Reproducibility: X/10\n- Overall (R1): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n","agentId":"a593143090e14f71f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed to write a thorough R1 review focused on Methods and Experiments. Let me compose it.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to decompose variance in LLM evaluation across infrastructure (numerical precision, GPU hardware), methodology (prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, option ordering), and measurement (test items) facets, with model treated as a crossed random facet. Nine experiments totaling ~4.3M item-level records across 4 models (7-9B), 2 models (14B), 5 benchmarks, and 2 GPU types yield three claims: (1) item selection dominates single-model variance but model-by-item interaction emerges as the second-largest source in cross-model analysis, with a monotonic cross-benchmark gradient; (2) infrastructure-methodology separability holds under binary scoring on knowledge benchmarks; (3) reproducibility budgets are benchmark-specific and metric-conditional, with G-theory requiring 4.4x more items than IRT. The experimental scope is ambitious and the methodological defense package is unusually thorough for this literature.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated factorial design.** The fully crossed design on MMLU (4 models x 3 precisions x 6 prompts x 6 seeds x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings x 200 items) is the most comprehensive variance decomposition in the LLM evaluation literature. The decision to cross infrastructure with methodology factors -- rather than studying them in isolation as all prior work has done -- directly tests an assumption the field has been making implicitly. This is sound experimental logic.\n\n2. **Appropriate statistical framework with correct implementation.** Henderson's Method III for balanced designs and REML for unbalanced designs are the right choices. The code (`variance_decomposition.py`) correctly implements the EMS equations: 2-way interactions are estimated first (MS_ij - MS_res divided by the product of levels of all other facets), then main effects are estimated accounting for interaction contributions. The negative-variance truncation to zero is standard practice. The D-study partition (fixed facets to universe score, random facets to error) is correctly implemented in `d_study.py`.\n\n3. **Comprehensive sensitivity and robustness analyses (MD1-MD9).** The 9 methodological defenses address the most predictable criticisms with quantitative evidence rather than hand-waving. Particularly strong: (a) the null Bernoulli simulation showing item_id = 0.08% vs. observed 61.1% (671x ratio), which rules out the mechanical variance explanation; (b) the REML vs. Henderson III comparison (max divergence 1.4%, rank order preserved); (c) the bootstrap vs. jackknife CI comparison (width ratio 0.96-1.04); (d) the fixed vs. random sensitivity analysis (G ranges 0.618-0.664). This is an exemplary robustness package.\n\n4. **Item resampling stability (Exp 2) directly addresses a critical threat.** Three independent stratified 200-item draws with item_id CV = 0.039 (well below the 15% threshold) and preserved rank order (Friedman p > 0.10) is strong evidence that the headline findings are not artifacts of the specific item sample. This experiment alone addresses one of the most common criticisms of subsampled evaluation studies.\n\n5. **Cross-benchmark gradient is convincing and explanatory.** The monotonic decrease of item_id from MMLU (60.4%) to MATH (22.1%) with Spearman rho = 1.0 across 5 benchmarks, accompanied by the inverse monotonic increase of prompt-by-item interaction (3.2% to 13.5%), provides an elegant reconciliation of seemingly contradictory findings in the literature. The 38.3pp spread is substantial and the pattern is internally consistent.\n\n6. **Power analysis preempts the \"underpowered\" criticism.** Power > 0.998 for effects >= 5% and MDE = 1.2% at 80% power, given the 891,200-record MMLU design, demonstrates that the near-zero infrastructure-methodology interactions are genuine nulls rather than failures to detect.\n\n7. **Model-as-facet analysis (Exp 1 cross-model) is a genuine methodological contribution.** The demonstration that item_id drops from 60.4% (single-model mean) to 36.8% (cross-model) while model-by-item absorbs 24.1% -- and that this collapses G from 0.618 to 0.065 -- is a clean and important finding. The D-study showing even 10 models yield G = 0.179 quantifies the irreducibility of this interaction.\n\n8. **The IRT vs. G-theory comparison (Exp 5) is a standalone contribution.** Showing that G-theory requires 4.4x more items (510 vs. 116) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores, and that G-theory G at IRT's recommended items is only 0.529, directly informs practitioners currently using tinyBenchmarks-style approaches.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Four models is marginal for random-effects estimation of the model facet (Major).** The model-as-facet analysis -- a core contribution -- estimates model-by-item variance from only 4 models (Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B). In G-theory practice, variance components from facets with fewer than 5-6 levels are known to be unstable and positively biased (Brennan, 2001, Ch. 4). The 95% CI on model-by-item [21.3, 27.0] is reasonably tight, but this tightness reflects bootstrap resampling over items, not over models -- the 4 models are fixed across all bootstrap samples. The actual uncertainty in the model-by-item estimate due to model sampling is not quantified. A leave-one-model-out analysis would partially address this: if removing any single model substantially changes the item_id / model-by-item ratio, the df=3 concern is not merely theoretical. The 14B extension (Exp 6) adds 2 models but in a separate analysis, not in the main crossed design, so it cannot strengthen the main estimate.\n\n2. **The \"precision\" facet conflates genuine numerical effects with vLLM engine artifacts (Major).** The paper uses enforce_eager=True for all precisions, which is good for controlling the engine confound discovered in the MVP. However, the memory file reveals that FP32 shows a consistent ~8-10pp accuracy degradation across all models. The paper attributes this to \"weights loaded from BF16 to FP32\" behavior, but this is a vLLM-specific code path issue, not a fundamental property of numerical precision. The precision facet thus has 3 levels where 2 (BF16, FP16) are nearly identical (<0.2pp difference) and 1 (FP32) is dramatically different due to an engine artifact. This means precision-by-item interaction (reported at ~7% on MMLU) is dominated by FP32's anomalous behavior. The LayerCast experiment (Exp 8) partially addresses this by showing precision drops from 1.4% to 0.5% with LayerCast, but the main analyses still use the confounded 3-level precision facet. The paper should present the bf16-vs-fp16-only decomposition as a sensitivity analysis to show how much the precision facet's contribution changes when FP32 is excluded.\n\n3. **Hardware crossing is severely underpowered (Moderate-Major).** Two GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell + one other) provide df=1 for the hardware facet. The reported hardware-by-methodology interaction of 0.18% [0.03, 0.42] is below the 0.5% threshold, but with 2 hardware levels, this estimate has almost no statistical power to detect anything. The key concern is that hardware-by-item = 1.2% [0.7, 1.8], which is non-negligible and exceeds hardware-by-methodology by 6.7x. With only 2 GPU types -- both NVIDIA data-center class -- the separability claim for hardware is much weaker than the paper implies. Cross-architecture effects (NVIDIA vs. AMD, consumer vs. data-center, different microarchitectures) could plausibly be larger. The paper should either (a) present the hardware separability finding as preliminary/suggestive rather than confirmatory, or (b) explicitly scope the claim to \"numerical precision separability\" and defer hardware to future work.\n\n4. **Option ordering facet is undefined for open-ended benchmarks (Moderate).** GSM8K and MATH are open-ended generation tasks without multiple-choice options. The paper reports 4 ordering levels for all benchmarks. What does \"option ordering\" mean for GSM8K and MATH? If it is not applicable and was held constant, then these benchmarks have a 5-facet rather than 6-facet design, which changes the residual interpretation and affects comparability across benchmarks. If some proxy was used (e.g., few-shot example ordering), this must be explicitly stated as it measures a different construct than MC option ordering. This is a non-trivial gap in the experimental description.\n\n5. **Temperature and ordering have too few design points for credible random-facet treatment (Moderate).** Temperature has 3 levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) and ordering has 4 levels (systematic rotations). G-theory requires that facet levels be a random sample from a well-defined universe of admissible conditions. Temperature values are not random draws from a continuous space -- they are selected boundary/midpoint values. The 4 orderings are all possible ABCD rotations, which exhausts the rotation universe but excludes permutations (24 total for 4 options). The sensitivity analysis (fixed vs. random: <3% change) partially mitigates this, but it does not resolve the interpretive problem: what \"universe\" are 3 temperatures generalizing to? The paper should discuss this more carefully in the Methods section and present the fixed-facet analysis as the primary result for these two facets.\n\n6. **D-study grid search procedure is underdocumented (Moderate).** The D-study projections (benchmark-specific budgets for G >= 0.80) are among the paper's most actionable outputs, yet the optimization procedure is not described. What is the objective function? Is it min total items subject to G >= 0.80, or min total conditions (items x prompts x seeds)? What are the search bounds and step sizes? Are costs considered? The `d_study.py` code reveals a simple univariate sweep (vary one facet 1-20, hold others at baseline) with a linear search for minimum replications -- this is not a joint optimization. If the reported budgets come from independent per-facet sweeps rather than joint optimization, the paper should state this explicitly, as jointly optimized allocations could be substantially different.\n\n7. **Cross-benchmark design asymmetry complicates comparison (Minor-Moderate).** MMLU uses 3 precisions x 3 temperatures while other benchmarks use 2 x 2. The paper reports a sensitivity analysis showing <5% change when MMLU is re-analyzed with matched 2x2, but the precision-related components on non-MMLU benchmarks are mechanically smaller because BF16 and FP16 produce nearly identical results (no FP32 in those designs). This means the \"cross-benchmark gradient\" for precision-related components is partly driven by design asymmetry rather than genuine benchmark differences. The item_id gradient is less affected because items vary independently of the precision design.\n\n8. **Gemma2 FP16 exclusion creates an unbalanced design (Minor).** Gemma2 cannot run FP16, so the cross-model analysis uses only BF16+FP32. This means the cross-model design is not fully balanced: Llama/Mistral/Qwen have 3 precision levels while Gemma has 2. Henderson's Method III assumes balanced designs. The paper states REML is used for unbalanced cases, which is correct, but the main MMLU analysis code (`variance_decomposition.py`) implements Henderson Method I (Type-I SS-based), not REML. The paper should clarify which estimator was actually used for the cross-model analysis and verify that the Gemma imbalance does not bias the results.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. What fraction of bootstrap resamples show model-by-item > item_id in the cross-model analysis? The CI on model-by-item [21.3, 27.0] and item_id [33.5, 40.2] do not overlap, which is reassuring, but the relative magnitude matters for the \"second-largest source\" claim.\n\n2. What happens to the variance decomposition if FP32 is excluded from the MMLU analysis, leaving only BF16 vs. FP16? This would isolate \"genuine\" numerical precision effects from the vLLM engine artifact. If precision-by-item drops from ~7% to near-zero, this substantially changes the story.\n\n3. How was option ordering operationalized for GSM8K and MATH? If these benchmarks lack MC options, the ordering facet either measures something different or was not varied, and this must be stated.\n\n4. Leave-one-model-out sensitivity: does removing any single model from the 4-model MMLU analysis change the model-by-item estimate by more than the bootstrap CI width?\n\n5. The D-study budgets assume practitioners will use multiple prompts and seeds. What is the D-study projection for the common practice of 1 prompt, 1 seed, varying only items? This would make the practical gap more concrete.\n\n6. For the fine-tuning experiment (Exp 7): seed-by-LR interaction dominates at 28.4%. Is this driven by specific seed-LR combinations that diverge (e.g., training instability), or is it a smooth interaction? A seed-LR interaction plot would help distinguish these scenarios.\n\n7. The Bernoulli null simulation (MD1) compares null item_id = 0.08% to observed 61.1%. But the relevant comparison for the model-by-item claim is whether a Bernoulli model with model-specific item difficulties (but no systematic model-by-item interaction beyond what Bernoulli mechanics produce) could generate the observed 24.1% model-by-item. Has this been checked?\n\n8. How sensitive is the cross-benchmark gradient to the specific 200-item samples? The item resampling stability test (Exp 2) was done only on MMLU. Given that GSM8K has much lower item_id (28.3%) and higher residual, sampling variability could be larger there.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The variance decomposition code (`variance_decomposition.py`) absorbs all 3-way and higher interactions into the residual. Exp 5 reports that higher-order interactions total 3.2%, with the largest being precision-by-temperature-by-item at 1.1%. It is unclear how this was computed -- the code only fits main effects and 2-way interactions. Was a separate analysis run for higher-order terms? If so, the code does not reflect this.\n\n2. The bootstrap resamples items with replacement but does not resample models. This means bootstrap CIs on model-by-item reflect item sampling uncertainty only, not model sampling uncertainty. This should be stated explicitly.\n\n3. The paper claims \"first multi-benchmark G-theory study\" in LLM evaluation. Verify this against Mesting (2026), who applied G-theory to MMLU and AILuminate (which could be considered multi-benchmark).\n\n4. The `text_exact_match` metric definition uses the most frequent generated text across all conditions as the \"canonical\" answer. This is a somewhat arbitrary reference point that depends on the design itself (adding more conditions could change the canonical text). An alternative would be the T=0, seed=42 output, which is design-independent.\n\n5. Logprob scoring (Exp 5): item_id = 53.2% vs. 60.4% for binary, with cross-metric rank correlation 0.94. This is reported as supporting metric robustness, but the 7.2pp difference in item_id share is non-trivial. The paper should discuss whether this difference is meaningful or within expected variation.\n\n6. The total record count of 4.29M should be broken down by experiment in a summary table for reproducibility. The current presentation makes it difficult to verify the total.\n\n7. Several numbers in the paper summary differ slightly from the project files (e.g., item_id = 60.4% in the summary vs. 61.1% in earlier project files). This likely reflects the transition from 2-model to 4-model single-model averages. Ensure internal consistency.\n\n8. The G coefficient formula should explicitly state whether relative G (Eρ^2) or absolute G (Φ) is used. The D-study code computes relative G (sigma_tau / (sigma_tau + sigma_delta)), which is confirmed by the partition rule, but this should be stated in the Methods.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 7/10\n- Experimental Design: 8/10\n- Reproducibility: 7/10\n- Overall (R1): 7/10\n- Confidence: 4/5 (familiar with G-theory, ANOVA-based variance decomposition, and LLM evaluation methodology)\n\n**Rationale:** The experimental design is the paper's greatest strength -- the factorial crossing of infrastructure with methodology across multiple benchmarks is novel, the scale is impressive, and the sensitivity analyses are exemplary. The statistical methodology is largely correct, with Henderson III and REML properly applied and bootstrap CIs computed. The main concerns are: (a) the model facet has only 4 levels, which is marginal for random-effects estimation of the headline model-by-item finding; (b) the FP32 engine artifact conflates precision effects with software behavior; (c) the hardware crossing with only 2 GPU types is too thin to support separability claims; and (d) the option ordering facet is undefined for open-ended benchmarks. None of these are fatal -- they are addressable through additional analysis (leave-one-model-out, FP32-excluded sensitivity, explicit scoping of hardware claims, and clarification of ordering for open-ended tasks). The cross-benchmark variance gradient, the IRT vs. G-theory comparison, and the model-as-facet demonstration are all well-supported and constitute genuine contributions. The paper sits at the \"good paper, accept\" threshold with required revisions."}],"totalDurationMs":182624,"totalTokens":86120,"totalToolUseCount":27,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":731,"cache_read_input_tokens":80985,"output_tokens":4401,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":731},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4401,"cache_read_input_tokens":80985,"cache_creation_input_tokens":731,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":731,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":16,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
